    "evidence_text": "detection methods perform in a cross-lingual setting, as the multilingual datasets available today are relatively small, and specific to a single target , .\n\nFurthermore, specific models tend to be developed for each single target or pair of targets .\n\nConcerns have been raised that cross-target performance is often considerably lower than fully supervised performance .\n\nIn this paper we propose a much larger dataset that combines multilinguality and a multitude of topics and targets.\n\nQuestions are available in four languages: English, Swiss Standard German, French, and Italian.\n\nThe language of a comment depends on the candidate's region of origin.\n\nWe have extracted the data from the voting advice application Smartvote.\n\nThey can also submit a free-text comment in order to justify, explain or differentiate their categorical answer.\n\nAn example is given in Figure FIGREF1.\n\nThe dataset\n\nis split into a multilingual training set and into multiple test sets to evaluate zero-shot cross-lingual and cross-target transfer.\n\nTo provide a baseline, we fine-tune a multilingual Bert model  on x-stance.\n\nWe show that the baseline accuracy is comparable to previous stance detection benchmarks while leaving ample room for improvement.\n\nIn addition, multilingual Bert can generalize to a degree both cross-lingually and in a cross-target setting.\n\nWe have made the dataset and the code for reproducing the baseline model publicly available.\n\nBoth are a collection of annotated Spanish and Catalan tweets.\n\nWith regard to the languages covered by x-stance, only monolingual datasets seem to be available.\n\nFor French, a collection of tweets on French presidential candidates has been annotated with stance .\n\nFor German, there is no known stance detection dataset, but\n\nnot further preprocess the text of the comments.\n\nWe used the langdetect library .\n\nWe applied the identifier in a two-step approach.\n\nIn the second run, drawing from these conclusions, we restricted the identifier's output to English, French, German and Italian.\n\nclass that is most frequent for a given target question.\n\nThe latter can only be applied to the supervised test set.\n\nWithin the field of stance detection, Bert can outperform both feature-based and other neural approaches in a monolingual English setting .\n\nWe follow the procedure outlined by  for such tasks.\n\nThe final hidden state corresponding to [CLS] is then classified by a linear layer.\n\nBaseline Experiments ::: Multilingual BERT Baseline ::: Training\tWe upsampled the\n\n`favor' class so that the two classes are balanced when summing over all questions and topics.\n\nA dropout probability of 0.1 was set on all layers.\n\nBaseline Experiments ::: Multilingual BERT Baseline ::: Results\tTable TABREF36 shows the results for the cross-lingual setting.\n\nM-Bert performs consistently better than the majority class baselines.\n\nEven the zero-short performance in Italian, while significantly lower than the supervised\n\nscores, is much better than the target-wise majority class baseline.\n\nResults for the cross-target setting are given in Table TABREF37.\n\nFurthermore, the cross-question score of M-Bert is slightly lower than the cross-topic score.\n\nBaseline Experiments ::: How Important is Consistent Language?\n\nAn alternative concept is vertical language consistency, whereby the questions are consistently presented in one language, regardless of the comment.\n\nWe chose English as a lingua franca because it had the largest share of data during the pretraining of M-Bert.\n\nWhile the effect is negligible in most settings, the cross-lingual performance clearly increases when all questions are\n\ngiven in English.\n\nBaseline Experiments ::: How Important are the Segments?\n\nOnly use the question and remove the comment (missing comments).\n\nIn both cases the performance decreases across all evaluation settings (Table TABREF39).\n\nThe effect of missing questions is especially strong in the supervised and cross-lingual settings.\n\nOn the other hand, the effect of omitting the questions is less pronounced in the cross-target settings.\n\nThe above\n\nsingle-segment experiments tell us that both the comment and the question provide crucial information.\n\nWe make sure that the random questions come from the same test set and language as the original questions.\n\nAgain we shuffle the comments only within test set boundaries.\n\nBaseline Experiments ::: How Important are Spelled-Out Targets?\n\nNamely, an alternative is to represent the target with a trainable embedding instead of a question.\n\nSegment A is then set to this symbol instead\n\nof a natural language question.\n\nAn M-Bert model that learns target embeddings instead of encoding a question performs clearly worse in the supervised and cross-lingual settings.\n\nFrom this we conclude that spelled-out natural language questions provide important linguistic detail that can help in stance detection.\n\nIt is interesting to see what errors the M-Bert model makes.\n\nTable TABREF47 presents instances where\n\nunseen languages and to unseen targets (both within a topic and to unseen topics).\n\nHowever, there is some gap in performance that future work could address.\n\nWe would like to thank Isabelle Augenstein for helpful feedback.\n\nMainly data pertaining to national-level issues were included to reduce variability.\n\nThe questions have also been translated into English.",
